# RFC 8288 Link response headers for agent / machine discovery.
# Astro copies this file from public/ into dist/client and Cloudflare's static
# asset handler applies it. Cloudflare is the only deploy target; the file was
# previously kept platform-neutral for Netlify, which is no longer in the
# request path.
# Relation types are IANA-registered and point to resources that exist:
#   describedby -> /llms.txt   (LLM-readable description of this site, RFC 8288)
#   service-doc -> /docs       (human-oriented documentation, RFC 8631)
/*
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain", </docs>; rel="service-doc"; type="text/html"

# Markdown twins. Every page is also published as agent-facing markdown at the
# same path with ".md" appended:
#   /about    -> /about.md
#   /docs/cli -> /docs/cli.md
#   /         -> /index.md
#
# Content-Type is pinned because Cloudflare serves .md as bare "text/markdown"
# with NO charset, and markdown has no in-band way to declare its encoding. The
# docs bodies contain real UTF-8 (arrows, typographic quotes), so without this a
# browser decodes them as Latin-1 and renders mojibake.
#
# Measured trade-off, not a guess: with this line, a request for a .md that does
# not exist returns HTTP 404 with an HTML body still labelled text/markdown,
# because Cloudflare matches these rules against the REQUEST path and applies
# them after resolving the 404 asset. That was verified on a preview deploy.
# Accepted, because the 404 status code is the primary signal and stays correct,
# whereas dropping this line mis-decodes all 232 valid markdown files. The clean
# fix for both is a markdown-aware 404, which needs a Worker.
#
# Cache-Control is safe under /*.md but must NEVER appear under /*: the
# Cloudflare adapter probes /_astro/probe against this file and skips injecting
# its own immutable cache block if any matching rule sets Cache-Control.
#
# X-Robots-Tag here is the DEFAULT, and the rules below lift it for every .md
# that is a twin of a real HTML page. See the block after this one.
/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Vary: Accept

# Twins point at their HTML page with rel="canonical", and drop the blanket
# noindex above.
#
# This is the "consolidate" strategy, not the "suppress" one, and the choice
# matters for an AEO site: noindex tells the answer engines we are trying to
# reach to skip the cleanest version of our own content, while a canonical
# resolves the duplicate without suppressing anything. Measured across the
# field: Vercel and Svelte send rel="canonical" and no noindex; Stripe and
# Firecrawl send noindex and no canonical; Cloudflare sends neither. Nobody
# sends both, because Google treats them as contradictory. We are in the first
# camp deliberately.
#
# Safe where the page-side rules were not: ".md" is itself the discriminator.
# No asset on this site ends in .md, so none of these can match /logo.png or
# /_astro/hash.css.
#
# NOT covered: /blueprints/:s1.md . That one path mixes 68 real twins with 56
# verbatim source downloads (<file-id>-semantic-blueprint.md) that have no HTML
# page at all, and _headers matches whole segments, so a rule cannot tell them
# apart. They keep the default noindex rather than being handed a canonical
# that 404s for 56 of them. The fix, if the 68 turn out to matter, is to emit
# the verbatim sources under their own prefix so the two become addressable.
/:s1.md
  Link: </:s1>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag
/docs/:s1.md
  Link: </docs/:s1>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag
/docs/:s1/:s2.md
  Link: </docs/:s1/:s2>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag
/blog/:s1.md
  Link: </blog/:s1>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag
/skills/:s1.md
  Link: </skills/:s1>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag
/blueprints/:s1/blueprint.md
  Link: </blueprints/:s1/blueprint>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag

# The root's twin is /index.md, whose canonical is "/" and not "/index".
# Placed after /:s1.md so that whichever rule Cloudflare resolves last wins.
/index.md
  Link: </>; rel="canonical"
  ! X-Robots-Tag

# There is deliberately NO per-page "Link: rel=alternate" rule here.
#
# An earlier revision carried /:s1/, /:s1/:s2/ and /:s1/:s2/:s3/ rules that
# advertised each page's markdown twin as an HTTP header. They depended on the
# trailing slash as the only thing separating a page URL from an asset URL:
# every page ended in "/", no asset did. Canonical URLs are now slash-less
# (trailingSlash:'never' in astro.config.mjs), so those patterns would match
# /logo.png and /_astro/hash.css, and the slashed forms they did match no longer
# resolve. A scoped rewrite (per-section prefixes plus an enumerated list of
# root-level pages) was rejected: it fails silently the first time someone adds
# a top-level page.
#
# Nothing is lost by this. The <link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown"> in
# SEO.astro reaches every page, applies the same hasMarkdownTwin() guard these
# rules could not express, and is the form crawlers actually parse.
